issues.body:
### Describe the feature or problem you'd like to solve

After compaction, the model no longer receives the `additionalContext` that `sessionStart` hooks returned, and nothing restores it until the session is resumed.

### Proposed solution

Any one of these would solve it:

1. When compaction replaces the history, keep the message that carries the `sessionStart` context, as the runtime already keeps the system message.
2. Run `sessionStart` again after compaction with `source: "compact"` and inject its `additionalContext`. Claude Code does this, so plugins that register `SessionStart` with a `compact` matcher would work on Copilot unchanged.
3. Add a `postCompact` hook whose `additionalContext` reaches the compacted context. #4441 proposes something close to this.

If the current behavior stays, please state in the hooks reference that `sessionStart` context does not survive compaction.

Plugins and repositories use `sessionStart` to inject rules that should hold for the whole session. Today the rules hold only until the first compaction, and neither the user nor the agent is told that they are gone.

### Example prompts or workflows

1. A plugin injects routing rules at session start, such as which workflow to load for multi-file changes, and expects them to hold for the whole session. Today, automatic compaction in a long autopilot session drops them, and the agent stops following them.
2. A `sessionStart` hook injects the models the user approved for subagents, and a `preToolUse` hook denies any other model. After compaction the agent no longer sees the list, picks another model, and the hook denies the call.
3. A repository hook injects ticket constraints at session start. After the user runs `/compact`, the constraints are gone until the session is resumed.

### Additional context

The current behavior matches the hooks reference. It says `sessionStart` fires when "A new or resumed session begins", lists its `source` values as `"startup"`, `"resume"`, and `"new"`, and says the runtime does not process `preCompact` output. So I'm filing this as a feature request, not a bug.

#### What I measured

I pointed a session at a local mock OpenAI-compatible server through a BYOK `provider` and recorded which markers each model request contained. A `sessionStart` hook returned one marker in `additionalContext`, and `copilot-instructions.md` in the config directory held another.

| Request | sessionStart context | copilot-instructions.md |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Turn 1 | yes | yes |
| Compaction summary request | yes | yes |
| Turn 2, after compaction | no | yes |
| Turn 3, after resume | yes | yes |

- `sessionStart` ran with `source: "new"` and then `source: "resume"`. It did not run for compaction.
- The runtime sends the hook context as a separate user message right after the system message. The first request after compaction contains only the system message, the summary message, and the new prompt.
- A plugin command hook and an SDK `onSessionStart` callback behaved the same. So did manual compaction and threshold-triggered background compaction.
- Runtime 1.0.99-2 and 1.0.100-1 on macOS arm64 gave the same result. The release notes through 1.0.103-0 don't mention a change.
- The summarizer may paraphrase the hook text into the summary, but the original text is gone.

The GitHub Copilot app shows the same pattern in normal use. The app shuts a session down about 10 minutes after the last turn and resumes it on the next message. That resume is the only point where `sessionStart` runs again, so after each compaction the context stays missing until the next idle resume.

Both workarounds I know of have costs. Copying the rules into `~/.copilot/copilot-instructions.md` works because that file stays in the system message, but every user has to edit a personal file, and the copy goes stale when the plugin changes. A `userPromptTransformed` hook could append the rules to every prompt, but the docs say the replaced content is stored in session history, so the rules would repeat on every turn. I haven't tried this one.

#### References, checked 2026-10-22

- Copilot hooks reference: https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/hooks-reference
- Claude Code hooks reference, where `SessionStart` `source` includes `compact`: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks
- Related issues: #4441, #4687
- #1138 asked for `preCompact` so hooks could refresh context before compaction. It was closed when `preCompact` shipped, but the runtime does not process `preCompact` output, so that use case is still not covered.

<details>
<summary>Repro script</summary>

Requires Node.js. Set `COPILOT_SDK` to the SDK's `index.js`. I used the copy bundled with the GitHub Copilot app. Set `COPILOT_RUNTIME` to a copilot runtime binary or to the `index.js` of the `@github/copilot` package. The session uses a local mock model server and a temporary config directory, and the script deletes the directory at the end.

```js
// Shows whether sessionStart additionalContext reaches the model after compaction.
//
// Usage:
//   COPILOT_SDK=/path/to/copilot-sdk/index.js \
//   COPILOT_RUNTIME=/path/to/copilot \
//   node repro-sessionstart-compaction.mjs
//
// COPILOT_RUNTIME is a runtime binary or the index.js of the @github/copilot package.
// The session talks to a local mock model server, so the script makes no real model calls.
// It writes only to a temporary COPILOT_HOME, which it deletes at the end.

import fs from "node:fs";
import http from "node:http";
import os from "node:os";
import path from "node:path";
import { pathToFileURL } from "node:url";

const { COPILOT_SDK, COPILOT_RUNTIME } = process.env;
if (!COPILOT_SDK || !COPILOT_RUNTIME) {
	console.error("Set COPILOT_SDK to the SDK's index.js and COPILOT_RUNTIME to the copilot runtime.");
	process.exit(2);
}
const { CopilotClient, RuntimeConnection, approveAll } = await import(pathToFileURL(COPILOT_SDK).href);

const HOOK_MARKER = "HOOK-MARKER-7Q2";
const INSTRUCTIONS_MARKER = "INSTRUCTIONS-MARKER-4K9";
const SUMMARY = [
	"<overview>A short test conversation.</overview>",
	"<history>1. The user sent a test message.</history>",
	"<work_done>None.</work_done>",
	"<technical_details>None.</technical_details>",
	"<important_files>None.</important_files>",
	"<next_steps>Wait for the next message.</next_steps>",
	"<checkpoint_title>Test checkpoint</checkpoint_title>",
].join("\n");

const tmp = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), "sessionstart-compaction-"));
const home = path.join(tmp, "home");
const work = path.join(tmp, "work");
fs.mkdirSync(home);
fs.mkdirSync(work);
fs.writeFileSync(path.join(home, "copilot-instructions.md"), `${INSTRUCTIONS_MARKER}\n`);

// A mock OpenAI-compatible server that records which markers each chat request contains.
const requests = [];
let step = "setup";
const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
	let raw = "";
	req.on("data", (chunk) => (raw += chunk));
	req.on("end", () => {
		if (req.method !== "POST") {
			res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "application/json" });
			res.end(JSON.stringify({ object: "list", data: [{ id: "mock-model", object: "model", owned_by: "mock" }] }));
			return;
		}
		const body = JSON.parse(raw);
		const messages = JSON.stringify(body.messages);
		const isSummary = messages.includes("Please create a detailed summary");
		requests.push({
			request: isSummary ? "compaction summary request" : step,
			"sessionStart context": messages.includes(HOOK_MARKER) ? "yes" : "no",
			"copilot-instructions.md": messages.includes(INSTRUCTIONS_MARKER) ? "yes" : "no",
		});
		const text = isSummary ? SUMMARY : "OK";
		const created = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
		const usage = { prompt_tokens: 1000, completion_tokens: 20, total_tokens: 1020 };
		if (!body.stream) {
			res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "application/json" });
			res.end(JSON.stringify({ id: "mock", object: "chat.completion", created, model: "mock-model", choices: [{ index: 0, message: { role: "assistant", content: text }, finish_reason: "stop" }], usage }));
			return;
		}
		res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "text/event-stream", "Cache-Control": "no-cache" });
		const send = (chunk) => res.write(`data: ${JSON.stringify(chunk)}\n\n`);
		send({ id: "mock", object: "chat.completion.chunk", created, model: "mock-model", choices: [{ index: 0, delta: { role: "assistant", content: text }, finish_reason: null }] });
		send({ id: "mock", object: "chat.completion.chunk", created, model: "mock-model", choices: [{ index: 0, delta: {}, finish_reason: "stop" }], usage });
		res.end("data: [DONE]\n\n");
	});
});
await new Promise((resolve) => server.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", resolve));
const { port } = server.address();

const sources = [];
const sessionConfig = {
	model: "mock-model",
	provider: { type: "openai", wireApi: "completions", baseUrl: `http://127.0.0.1:${port}/v1`, apiKey: "mock", maxPromptTokens: 120000, maxOutputTokens: 4096 },
	onPermissionRequest: approveAll,
	workingDirectory: work,
	hooks: {
		onSessionStart: async (input) => {
			sources.push(input.source);
			return { additionalContext: `${HOOK_MARKER} (source: ${input.source})` };
		},
	},
};
const newClient = () =>
	new CopilotClient({
		connection: RuntimeConnection.forStdio({ path: COPILOT_RUNTIME }),
		baseDirectory: home,
		workingDirectory: work,
		logLevel: "error",
	});

let client = newClient();
try {
	await client.start();
	const session = await client.createSession(sessionConfig);
	step = "turn 1";
	await session.sendAndWait({ prompt: "First message." }, 60000);
	step = "manual compaction";
	await session.rpc.history.compact({ trigger: "manual" });
	step = "turn 2, after compaction";
	await session.sendAndWait({ prompt: "Second message." }, 60000);
	await session.disconnect();
	await client.stop();

	client = newClient();
	await client.start();
	const resumed = await client.resumeSession(session.sessionId, sessionConfig);
	step = "turn 3, after resume";
	await resumed.sendAndWait({ prompt: "Third message." }, 60000);
	await resumed.disconnect();
} finally {
	await client.stop().catch(() => {});
	server.close();
	fs.rmSync(tmp, { recursive: true, force: true });
}

console.log(`sessionStart ran with source: ${sources.join(", ")}`);
console.table(requests);
process.exit(0);
```

</details>

## Summary

Built-in websearch providers participate in the HTTP-429 → `Retry-After` cooldown → switch-to-another-available-provider path, but custom providers registered via `ctx.websearch.transform` have no way to signal a rate limit: any `execute()` failure surfaces as `ServiceUnavailableError` and hard-fails the search. A pinned plugin provider therefore loses the failover story built-ins get for free.

## Environment

- opencode version: 2.1.2
- OS: Darwin 25.6.0 (darwin arm64)
- Terminal: ghostty (`TERM=xterm-ghostty`)
- Shell: /bin/zsh
- Install/channel: release (Homebrew tap, `channel=latest`)
- Active plugins: custom websearch provider via `ctx.websearch.transform` (plus two unrelated plugins)

## Reproduction

1. Register a websearch provider whose `execute()` throws an error carrying `.status = 429`:

```js
export default {
  id: "failtest",
  async setup(ctx) {
    await ctx.websearch.transform((editor) => {
      editor.add({
        id: "fail429",
        name: "Fail 429",
        execute: async () => {
          const e = new Error("forced 429");
          e.status = 429;
          throw e;
        },
      });
    });
  },
};
```

2. Pin it in config: `"websearch": { "provider": "fail429" }` (with `TAVILY_API_KEY` present so a healthy built-in provider is available).
3. Trigger a search: `opencode api post /api/websearch -d '{"query":"anything"}'`.

Observed response:

```json
{"_tag":"ServiceUnavailableError","message":"Web search request failed: fail429","service":"fail429"}
```

No retry occurs on the available tavily provider. A plain `throw new Error(...)` behaves identically.

## Expected Behavior

A documented error contract that a custom provider can use to signal rate limiting — e.g. a typed `WebSearch.RateLimited { retryAfter? }` error (or honoring `status`/`retryAfter` on thrown errors) — which the provider selector treats with the same cooldown + switch-provider behavior it applies to built-ins.

## Actual Behavior

Plugin errors are opaque: wrapped as `ServiceUnavailableError`, request fails. The 429 handling ("Web search rate limited (HTTP 429)" status map + Retry-After cooldown) exists only inside the built-in providers' own HTTP call path; the `WebSearch.*` error taxonomy exposes `ProviderRequired`/`ProviderNotFound` but no rate-limited kind.

## Additional Context

- `"random"` selection also appears to consider built-ins only: with a custom provider registered and a key present, random picked a built-in 4/4 times — custom providers currently participate only via explicit pinning.
- Current workaround we ship: the plugin itself catches Brave failures and queries the Tavily REST API as a fallback. It works, but every provider-plugin author would re-implement core failover/cooldown logic with no shared state.
- Docs nicety: state which selection/failover semantics apply to plugin-registered providers.

### What happened?

With a `sessionId`, the faux provider simulates prompt caching, but the tokens it reports as `cacheWrite` are also left in `input`. `input + cacheRead + cacheWrite` is larger than the prompt it estimated: a 1002-token prompt is reported as 2004 on the first request, and a 1107-token prompt as 1213 on the second.

`withUsageEstimate()` in `packages/ai/src/providers/faux.ts`:

```ts
cacheWrite = Math.ceil((promptLength - cachedChars) / 4);
input = Math.max(0, promptTokens - cacheRead);   // cacheWrite not subtracted
} else {
cacheWrite = promptTokens;                        // input stays promptTokens
```

### Steps to reproduce

```ts
import { createModels } from "@earendil-works/pi-ai/models";
import { fauxAssistantMessage, fauxProvider, fauxText } from "@earendil-works/pi-ai/providers/faux";

const faux = fauxProvider();
const models = createModels();
models.setProvider(faux.provider);
faux.setResponses([fauxAssistantMessage([fauxText("ok")])]);
const model = models.getModel("faux", "faux-1")!;
const reply = await models.complete(
	model,
	{ messages: [{ role: "user", content: "x".repeat(4000), timestamp: 0 }] },
	{ sessionId: "s1" },
);
const { input, cacheRead, cacheWrite } = reply.usage;
console.log({ input, cacheRead, cacheWrite }); // { input: 1002, cacheRead: 0, cacheWrite: 1002 }
```

### Expected behavior

`input` excludes written tokens, as with real providers: `input = promptTokens - cacheRead - cacheWrite` (0 on the first request). 

### Version

1.4.0

`pi.exec()` returns `{stdout: "", stderr: "", code: 1, killed: false}` both for a command that is not installed and for a command that exits 1 with no output, so an extension cannot tell "binary missing" from "ran and found nothing". Validated with `pi -ne` and a single inline extension (no other extensions loaded), Pi 1.3.0.

Repro:

```ts
// /tmp/probe.ts
import { writeFileSync } from "node:fs";
import type { ExtensionAPI } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

export default function probe(pi: ExtensionAPI) {
  pi.on("session_start", async () => {
    const missing = await pi.exec("definitely-not-a-command-xyz", [], { timeout: 5000 });
    const silentExit = await pi.exec("/bin/false", [], { timeout: 5000 });
    writeFileSync("/tmp/pi-exec-probe.json", JSON.stringify({ missing, silentExit }, null, 2));
  });
}
```

```bash
pi -ne -e /tmp/probe.ts --no-session -p "Reply with exactly: ok"
cat /tmp/pi-exec-probe.json
# both entries: {"stdout":"","stderr":"","code":1,"killed":false}
```

Cause: `packages/coding-agent/src/core/exec.ts` does

```ts
waitForChildProcess(proc).catch((_err) => resolve({ stdout, stderr, code: 1, killed }));
```

`waitForChildProcess` itself rejects correctly (`ENOENT: spawn definitely-not-a-command-xyz ENOENT`); only the `catch` discards it (the guard came in with 25b185f3 for the Windows hang fix).

Expected: keep the message observable, e.g. `resolve({ stdout, stderr: stderr || message, code: 1, killed })` (a distinct code such as 127 would also work), plus a regression test asserting `stderr` matches `/ENOENT/` for a missing binary.

Impact: `execCommand`'s only caller is `core/extensions/loader.ts` (the extension `pi.exec` API); built-in tools already surface spawn errors. In practice an `ast-grep`-style tool that treats exit 1 with empty stderr as "no matches" reports "no results" whenever the binary is missing — a silent false negative.

## What happens
Regular expressions used in while loops on user-controlled text can exhibit catastrophic backtracking (ReDoS). Patterns like `(a+)+` or nested quantifiers on malicious input cause exponential time complexity, freezing the event loop.

