Thanks to @KrasimirKralev for the fix and its regression coverage.

Merge danger: two-way door; reverting restores permissive CLI input handling without migrating state or changing valid provider requests. Blast radius is malformed image geometry input before generation/edit dispatch. Existing valid ratio mapping and output behavior are retained.

Closes #179158

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes: while an OpenAI-compatible (Chat Completions), Azure Responses, ChatGPT Responses or Kimi model streams a large tool call, the Gateway re-parses the whole argument buffer on almost every streamed chunk, so one 60 KB file write costs more than a second of extra Gateway CPU.

## User Impact

User impact: coding-agent turns that write or edit large files on these providers reach the tool and the reply sooner, and stop taking that CPU away from every other session on the same Gateway. Arguments, repairs, warnings and the final tool call are unchanged.

## Why This Change Was Made

The malformed tool-call argument repair wrapper (#42835, #43824) runs `tryExtractUsableToolCallArguments` on the whole buffer whenever a delta contains `}` or `]`, and on every short delta once a closer has been seen. Each attempt parses the buffer with `JSON.parse`, scans it for a balanced object from the start, and looks for smart quotes. Every buffer the function can accept, though, ends with the object's `}` followed only by whitespace and at most three allowed suffix characters. That holds for preserved JSON (`safeParseJsonRecord`), for a balanced prefix, and for a smart-quoted object (`readCommaSeparatedBody` returns just past its `}`); both repairs then pass the suffix through `finishToolCallArgumentRepair`. While the buffer ends inside a string or after a comma, the attempt cannot succeed.

- `tryExtractUsableToolCallArguments` now rejects a buffer that does not end in that shape before any parsing (`endsWithRepairableObjectClose`). Attempts still run on the same deltas, so a cached repair is still dropped on the delta that invalidates it (#43824), and the per-delta semantics asked for on #113125 stay.
- The short-delta trigger tested `/[}\]]/` against the whole buffer each time; it now keeps a per-call `sawCloser` flag in the same state object, which is replaced or deleted wherever the buffer is reset.

The second commit extends the committed chat-turn workload, because `scripts/bench-channel-turn.mts` used the Responses API, which this wrapper never wraps. `--tool-args-chars N` moves the mock provider to Chat Completions, gives each turn its own session, and streams a `write` call carrying N characters, 4 at a time, through a new `toolCall` response control in `scripts/e2e/mock-openai-server.mjs` (with a test). The default workload is unchanged.

Size: production +20/-7 in one file. Tests: one `it.each` row that fails if the new gate becomes stricter than the suffix rule (it fails with an off-by-one strip limit and without the inner `trimEnd`), and one mock-server test. Workload: +102/-9 across the bench, its doc and the mock. No test asserts parse counts; the counts below are the evidence, as `.agents/skills/openclaw-performance/SKILL.md` asks.

## Evidence

