## What Problem This Solves

Fixes ordinary chat text being masked after provider authentication returns ordinary HTTP metadata. A successful request can still leave altered chat history; successful delegation does not disprove this separate data-integrity problem.

## User Impact

Recognized HTTP metadata no longer becomes a process-wide secret merely because it appears in prepared request headers. The fix preserves ordinary reply text and keeps genuine credential controls protected. No configuration or storage migration is introduced. It prevents new false registrations; it does not clear an already-populated running registry or restore previously masked history.

## Why This Change Was Made

The generic prepared-auth boundary currently treats every header value as a credential. This patch keeps classification in the existing model-provider header-policy owner and exempts only a bounded set of recognized metadata names. It deliberately does **not** invert the existing sensitive-name heuristic: an unknown custom header remains protected.

Explicit API keys and auth are protected first. Values classified as credentials in the current prepared result are also retained in a per-call Set, independently of the logging registry’s six-character minimum and bounded capacity. Short API-key, bearer/header-auth, and custom-header duplicates therefore remain on the credential-protection path even under metadata names; actual sealing still follows the existing sentinel-mode setting. Already registered values, explicit header auth, and existing sentinels are not declassified by a metadata name. The shared literal matcher, logging-mode guarantees, storage redactor, and the output-credential protection from #104547 remain in place.

Scope: recognized transport metadata and the OpenAI organization-routing header (account metadata, not a claim of public-disclosure safety). Unrecognized provider-specific metadata remains protected; no MCP change, new public sensitivity API, registry redesign, or general redaction bypass is included.

